The documentation for the permissions API lists four levels on an AI/BI dashboard: CAN_READ (shown as Can View) views it; CAN_RUN also runs it; CAN_EDIT also edits the draft; CAN_MANAGE also changes its permissions. Legacy dashboards are a different object with their own permission model, so an access review names which kind it is reading. Editors work on the draft, viewers see the published version, and publishing decides whose data permissions apply, which the next beat takes up.

The second door is Unity Catalog, whose full model the governance track owns; three privileges are enough here. Reading a table needs `USE CATALOG` on its catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on its schema and `SELECT` on the table: `SELECT` alone opens nothing while either parent is closed.

```sql
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG quality_pilot TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA quality_pilot.accepted TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.publish_status TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
```

Whether a tile returns rows is decided by these privileges for whichever principal the query runs as. A page permission never grants a table privilege, and a table privilege never grants page access. The page reads these two objects, so Cinderline's matrix has two columns per principal: `north-reporting-analysts` gets Can View and the grants above, and must not reach raw or quarantine rows through any filter; `quality-stewards` get the same plus `USE SCHEMA` and `SELECT` on the quarantine table; the data team gets Can Edit and works with its own grants, never those of `sp-north-nightly`, the service principal that writes the accepted tables and holds no page permission, because the identity that writes the data is not the identity that shows it; everyone else gets nothing on either door. The page is published with individual data permissions, so these grants, not the publisher's, protect the rows.

How to review it: list the page's access control entries, run `SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE accepted.daily_line_rate` and the same for the view, then open the page as a test analyst and as an outsider and record what each sees. A viewer missing a grant should see the tile fail with a permission error rather than results (check the exact rendering in a workspace), which must never be read as the contract's "no inspections". What fails: granting Can Manage to fix a data error, or SELECT on a raw schema because a filter "needs it".
